  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe warm front that brought last night's rain fall will be into \nnorthern Maine this morning with the forecast area within the warm \nsector of an area of low pressure over Quebec. Overcast skies early \nthis morning will give way to mostly sunny skies by mid day. Surface \nheating combined with dew points in the mid 60s to near 70 degrees \nwill allow for surface based CAPE to build to over 1500 J/kg ahead \nof an approaching cold front. CAMs are in decent agreement that the \nfront will cross the mountains early afternoon and will push \noffshore this evening. Deep layer shear around 35 kts in\ncombination sufficient instability will allow for at least\nisolated thunderstorms this afternoon along the front with some\nstorms capable of becoming strong to severe with gusty winds and\nhail. CAMs continue to show very dry air above 700 mb that will\nbe hostile to updrafts so coverage will likely be limited for\nstorms. CAMs generally favor the mountains/foothills of Maine\ntowards central Maine for the best chances for storms with some\nchances extending southwestward through coastal Maine.\nThunderstorms will likely be pushing offshore around 6 PM.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nHigh pressure builds over the Northeast Thursday in the wake of\nWednesday cold front. A cooler and drier airmass will be in\nplace with highs ranging from the low 70s north to low 80s and \ndew points in the 40s and 50s. Pleasant weather will continue \nthrough the weekend with highs moderating into the low to mid \n80s and afternoon sea breezes likely along the coast. Ensembles \nshow troughing will deepen over the Northeast Monday into the \nmiddle of next week that will favor more in the way of shower \nchances.",
